I compiled the data deal rules people keep posting on here into one list, so you can check a company against all of them before you pitch it, with credit to whoever said each one

@troyaitken_ says these are the four things that kill a data licensing deal before it starts

Under 150GB total

Less than 3 years of history

No internal communications included

Numbers that don't survive verification

@Freeman_Lewin says your broker should be ingesting your data as-is, so you bring the setup and they handle the rest, and then turning that raw data into buy-ready bundles that are de-PII'd

He also says they should be breaking down what you have into what buyers actually pay for and rebundling those layers into recurring revenue instead of one-off licenses, because brokers that just make intros are selling you short

@mattceras thinks it's going to be kind of wild west-ish for a bit, with people helping companies sell all kinds of data mostly to the large AI labs and some licensors

His example is that if OpenAI has no intention of training models on cold calling, a smaller shop might go buy cold call transcripts from SaaS orgs to train their own model, and brokers could get involved in those niche mandates

@termsheetinator says he'd rather get on a sales call and find out what the business has, but when someone replies not interested you can send a short note with your referral link and 2-3 benefits and then leave them alone

If cash gets tight a month or two later and they apply through your link, you can still get paid as long as the referral qualifies

@RyanLockAI from Polyshares said about one of their brokers that volume is irrelevant when every meeting he books is a quality company from in-demand industries ready to progress a transaction, because lab budgets aren't infinite and slop is being competed away

He also shared that Polyshares partnered with @UseCorgi to protect Polyshares and its data partners from claims over how that data is used in AI training, so I'd bring that up when an owner asks about risk

@aliansarinik, the CEO of micro1, says the same person can show up across Slack, email, spreadsheets and databases, so if you anonymize each one separately the links between them start to break

He also says some of the most valuable training data for frontier models lives inside fully functioning companies, and micro1 has been getting over 100 companies a day signing up to license their data

If you want to capitalize on this, read my data broker article next because it has the whole process and the email I send owners
